<BaseLayout title="About" description="Austin Woyshnar — construction engineer in Boston, writing about regional economies, infrastructure, and institutions.">
  <section class="about-layout">
    <div class="about-lead"><span class="eyebrow">About</span><h1>I’m interested in the structures that make some places more capable of adapting than others.</h1></div>
    <div class="about-copy">
      <p>I am a Project Engineer at Thornton Tomasetti in Boston. I work on temporary works for contractors—shoring, crane pick and erection analysis, and equipment load path design: the engineering that holds a project up while it is being built.</p>
      <p>Behind that is roughly seven to eight years of combined structural design and field experience, and an MBA from Quantic School of Business &amp; Technology. The two halves ask a version of the same question from different sides—what a structure, or a region, can actually carry.</p>
      <p>I grew up in Scranton, Pennsylvania, and now work in Boston. The contrast between the two is what got me interested in the questions this site takes up: how cities accumulate institutions, skills, infrastructure, ownership, and opportunity—and what happens when those systems weaken or fail to renew themselves.</p>
      <p>This site is a home for that inquiry. I write about regional economies, public systems, industrial succession, infrastructure, and the institutional choices that influence where people can build a life.</p>
      <p>The work ranges from traditional essays to interactive visual models. The goal is not to make every question look simple. It is to make complicated relationships clear enough that a reader can inspect the argument and decide what they think.</p>
      <aside><span class="eyebrow">Working themes</span><ul><li>Cities and regional economies</li><li>Infrastructure and public investment</li><li>Institutions, ownership, and accountability</li><li>Industrial succession and adaptive capacity</li></ul></aside>
